[
"# Program Concept v2 — full depth, two lanes\n\n2026-09-26 · @Mohammad AlFalasi\n\n## The verdict on v0 and v1\n\nThe original syllabus (v0) has the right depth and the wrong delivery; the rebuild (v1) has the right delivery and the wrong depth; the fault in v1 was treating \"accessible\" as \"shorter\", when the real fix is to write once at full depth and let the learner choose how far in to go.\n\n| Dimension | v0 (original syllabus) | v1 (rebuild) | Keep for v2 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Depth and professional appeal | Postgraduate; studio-scale briefs; tools to fabrication level; venture realism | Entry level; 15-hour lenses; tools trimmed to a thread; venture to literacy | v0's depth, all of it, as the Mastery tier of every module |\n| Audience | Designers only, implicitly advanced | Designers plus the people who brief and judge | v1's two lanes |\n| Structure | Six tracks, duplicated tails, no outcomes or hours | Four levels, outcomes and hours, artifact-driven | v1's structure, holding v0's content |\n| Practice areas | Four studios: art, space, object, body | Six lenses, adding identity and digital product | Six areas, each with a lens (Practice) and a studio (Mastery) |\n",
"| Tools | Eleven modules, each a career | One free-tools thread, five electives dropped | Core thread at Practice; the dropped electives restored at Mastery with free tools |\n| Venture and release | Two tracks, 15 modules, near-verbatim duplication | Five short literacy modules | Five modules, Practice as literacy, Mastery as full simulations and a real handover package |\n| Register | Manifesto adjectives, dense, memorable, exclusionary | Plain, sometimes flat | Precise professional vocabulary, no adjectives; the rule in §4 |\n| Geography | Gulf, MENA, SWANA as core identity | Global first, region parked | Global spine with a regional slot in every unit; the regional vertical as the first expansion |\n| Delivery | Assumes cohorts, live crits, kits, specialists, budget | Self-paced, AI partner, no budget, free sources | v1's delivery, with the AI partner taking the fabricator, client and stranger roles the studios needed |\n| The artifact | Concept DNA plus anchor, family, dossier | Concept DNA, growing per unit, lane-specific additions | v1's growth model with v0's full dossier at Mastery |\n",
"| Assessment | Critical defence, dossier, live panel | Tests and drift scores, no grades | Both: tests at every depth, a defence at Mastery, still no grades |\n| Method (Track 02) | The strongest part, complete | Kept, lightened, given a data structure | v0's completeness on v1's data structure |\n\n**What was lost in v1 that mattered.** The studio-scale brief (a real site, a real object family, a capsule collection); the invisible systems taught to working depth (back of house, egress, BOM, tolerances, tech-packs); the tool electives; the sense that the program is for people who will actually build; and the regional specificity that made it unlike anything else.\n\n**What v0 lacked that v1 supplied.** A learner who is not a designer; outcomes, hours and sequence; the artifact as the through-line; free sources; a delivery that works without a room, a budget or a faculty; and two practice areas the industry commissions most.\n\n## The combining principle\n\n",
"Write every module once, at the depth of the original, and give it three depths of entry: Orientation (the lab, the vocabulary, one case: enough to see the idea), Practice (the professional content: enough to use and to brief it), and Mastery (the studio-scale brief, the tools, the defence: enough to build and be judged on it); the lanes do not change the content, they change the default depth and the Apply task.\n\n| Depth | What it contains | Time share | Who it is for by default |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Orientation | The provocation, the lab, the term named after the lab, one case, one short Apply | About 15% of a module | Anyone; the briefer's floor; a taster for makers |\n| Practice | The full concept set, the world set of cases, the method, the make or brief Apply, the partner's Mirror and Stranger | About 45% | Briefers' default; makers' minimum |\n| Mastery | The original's studio material: long-form briefs on a real site, object family, body or brand; tools electives; the invisible systems to working depth; the partner as Fabricator, Client and CFO; the defence | About 40% | Makers' default; briefers who commission at scale |\n\n",
"**Why this is not dumbing down.** The Mastery tier is the original curriculum, complete, in plainer language. Orientation is not a simpler version of it; it is the first fifteen minutes of it, designed so that a non-designer can start. The learner who stops at Orientation has met the real idea, not a cartoon of it.\n\n**Lanes on top of depths**\n\n|  | Make lane | Brief-and-judge lane |\n|---|---|---|\n| Default path | Practice through Foundations; Mastery through Method, one studio, the tools core and the defence | Orientation through Foundations; Practice through Method, one lens and the venture modules; Mastery for R3 (briefing and handover) |\n| Apply task | Produce | Write the brief, or judge a supplied piece with the four tests |\n| Artifact | DNA, anchor, adjacent translation, dossier | DNA, brief, judged set, commissioning record |\n| Can they cross? | Yes; any learner may take any depth of any module |  |\n\n",
"**What keeps the professional appeal.** The studio briefs at Mastery are the same ones a design master's would set; the world sets are large and global; the tools go to fabrication, capture and analytics; the venture modules run real numbers; the vocabulary is the profession's own; and the regional vertical, when built, gives the program a position nobody else holds. What makes it accessible is only the entry ramp and the removal of adjectives.\n\n## The v2 module map\n\nFour levels, 21 modules plus six tools electives; about 45 hours if taken at Orientation, about 200 at Practice with one area, and about 480 at Mastery with one studio, which is between a postgraduate certificate and a diploma in size. Hours are Orientation / Practice / Mastery.\n\n### Level 1 — Foundations\n\n| Module | Orientation | Practice | Mastery | Hours |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
"| F1 Histories of making | The long timeline in nine pivots; the reference-to-appropriation spectrum | Centres and peripheries across the globe; how histories are written; counter-histories; obscured labour; reading an object as history; the attribution and consultation protocol | A counter-history research piece on one lineage from primary sources, with an attribution record; the regional slot filled for the learner's own place | 4 / 12 / 16 |\n| F2 Form | Archetype DNA, grid, figure and ground, tension labs | Proportion systems across cultures (Islamic geometry, tatami and ken, classical and modular, textile grids, Chinese modular building); typology theory; scale; visual gravity | A typological subversion study built parametrically: one archetype, five subversions, identity kept, with the rule that generates them | 4 / 10 / 12 |\n| F3 Body and senses | Affordance and colour-interaction labs | The body as metric; cognitive load; colour physics and colour across cultures; light as hierarchy; thresholds and sequence | A full sensory system spec for the learner's world: anthropometric rules, palette and lighting logic, threshold sequence | 4 / 10 / 10 |\n",
"| F4 Material and meaning | Material-as-text and sign-swap labs | Provenance, lifecycle, patina; semiotics; language and script as structure across writing systems; tone of voice | Material dossier (five materials, lifecycle, meaning, ageing) and a multi-script text architecture | 4 / 10 / 10 |\n| F5 Systems and consequences | Ecosystem map and futures wheel | Feedback and stocks; non-human actors; lifecycle and planetary weight; scenario methods; what design cannot fix | Scenario set and first planetary ledger for the learner's intervention, with data | 3 / 8 / 10 |\n\n### Level 2 — Method (one continuous project)\n\n| Module | Orientation | Practice | Mastery | Hours |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| M1 Research and premise | Synthesis board and perimeter card | Research routes; credibility and bias; synthesis to insight; stance and contradiction; enable, protect, resist, reject, transform; non-goals; research ethics | A two-week field research sprint with archive work and interviews, to a defended World Premise | 3 / 10 / 12 |\n",
"| M2 Concept DNA | DNA Builder and the four tests on a supplied world | Constants, variables, thresholds; the twelve grammars and choosing among them; anti-DNA; alignment with practice, collection and masterbrand DNA; category obligations; the Stranger test; versioning | Full grammar set written out; three blind-interpretation rounds (peer, agent, both); contributor brief and agent prompt architecture; DNA v1.0 with change log | 4 / 14 / 16 |\n| M3 World architecture | Collection matrix | Roles; families, series, editions, drops; cross-scale coherence; distribution of cost and risk; pacing and curation; carriers and interfaces | Full collection map with risk and cost distribution, editorial calendar, carrier strategy | 3 / 10 / 12 |\n| M4 Make and test | Prototype question planner | Choosing practice and anchor; prototype fidelity; the adjacent translation; abandoned directions; revision history; specialist gaps | Anchor expression, differentiated family, adjacent translation, evidence compilation, DNA v2.0 | 2 / 10 / 30 |\n\n### Level 3 — Practice areas (each a lens at Practice, a studio at Mastery; take one or two)\n\n",
"| Area | Orientation | Practice (the lens) | Mastery (the studio, from the original units) | Hours |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| S1 Space and experience | What a space makes you do | Program before form; choreography; atmosphere; reuse; the invisible systems; plan and section; briefing a spatial designer | The ten units of 3.2 on a real base-build or reuse site: lineage and regional modernisms, programme and scenography, choreography, palimpsest, tectonics, microclimate, urban edge, back of house and life safety, economics, representation and digital twin | 2 / 15 / 70 |\n| S2 Object and product | What an object makes you do | The object as agent; hand and body; use choreography; cardboard prototyping; making routes; BOM and box; repair; briefing a product designer | The ten units of 3.3: ontology and craft, ergonomics, typology and proxemics, the buck, soft architecture, industrial methods, compliance, cyber-physical, supply chain, circularity; a resolved object family | 2 / 15 / 60 |\n",
"| S3 Body and wearables | What a garment makes you do | Silhouette and power; motion and ease; textile logic; pattern basics; semiotics of dress; collection matrix; tech-pack; labour; briefing a fashion designer | The twelve units of 3.4: from sartorial history to supply-chain ethics; a capsule with pattern, drape simulation, grading, tech-pack, campaign frame | 2 / 15 / 70 |\n| S4 Art and installation | What a room does to a work | The container; data to space; scale and site; light and sound; audience and time; documentation; funding; commissioning an artist | The eight units of 3.1: a site-responsive proposal with light and sound simulation, audience rules, decay plan, ecological ledger, funding defence | 2 / 15 / 50 |\n| S5 Identity and communication (new) | What a mark makes you feel | The brand as world; marks, type and colour as system; tone; grids; multi-script systems; campaign; the design brief | A complete identity system generated from the DNA and stress-tested across ten touchpoints, with guidelines a stranger can apply | 2 / 15 / 50 |\n",
"| S6 Digital product and interface (new) | What a screen makes you do | Interface as affordance; flows and states; design systems as DNA; content; accessibility; prototyping; the product brief | A working prototype with a design-token system derived from the DNA, an accessibility audit, and a handover spec | 2 / 15 / 50 |\n\n### Tools (core thread at Practice, electives at Mastery)\n\n| Module | Practice (thread) | Mastery (elective, 12–16 h each) |\n|---|---|---|\n| T1 Seeing and drawing | Gesture, line weight, plan, section, movement notation | Full orthographic and exploded sets of a real assembly |\n| T2 Parametric thinking | One rule, many outputs (p5.js) | Node-based definitions; form-finding by force (Blender geometry nodes; Grasshopper if licensed) |\n| T3 Working with AI | Prompt architecture; agent briefs; drift scoring | Agent teams; workflow architecture; bias and data-sovereignty review |\n| T4 Simulation | A scene, a light, a walk (three.js templates) | Real-time walkthrough; interface prototype; sound design in a DAW |\n| T5 Honest evidence | What a render claims (Blender) | Physically based materials; cinematography; render ethics audit |\n",
"| T6 Reality capture (elective) | — | Phone photogrammetry; point clouds (free tools) |\n| T7 Data visualisation (elective) | — | Parse, encode, spatialise a dataset |\n| T8 Physical computing (elective) | — | Sensor, actuator, behaviour; simulated hardware where no kit (free simulators) |\n| T9 Environmental analytics (elective) | — | Climate simulation and a full LCA (openLCA, free climate tools) |\n| T10 Fabrication (elective) | — | Toolpaths, additive constraints, failure analysis |\n\n### Level 4 — Venture and release\n\n| Module | Orientation | Practice | Mastery | Hours |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R1 The world as venture | Orbital map | Brand architecture; community and access; campaign as world extension | Full masterbrand and orbital architecture with rules of engagement; a campaign architecture | 2 / 8 / 10 |\n| R2 Money, law and rights | Runway simulator | Cash and burn; funding routes; IP taxonomy; attribution as protocol; responses to copying | 18-month model with two scenarios; IP and attribution protocol; contract anatomy (literacy, reviewed) | 2 / 8 / 12 |\n",
"| R3 Making it real | Collaboration matrix | Briefing others; minimum viable implementation; handover packages; specialist orchestration | A complete handover package for the studio project, audited; an MVI negotiation against the partner as fabricator and CFO | 2 / 8 / 12 |\n| R4 Responsibility | Lifecycle planner | Release, maintenance, repair, end of life; the planetary ledger | The final ledger with data; a repair and end-of-life design for the studio project | 2 / 6 / 8 |\n| R5 Defence and dossier | A transfer brief in miniature | The transfer brief; the stranger test at scale; dossier curation | A transfer brief under an unfamiliar constraint; a defence with the partner's panel; the professional dossier with abandoned directions and revision history | 2 / 8 / 20 |\n\n**Totals.** Orientation path about 45 h; Practice path with one area about 200 h; Mastery path with one studio and two tool electives about 480 h. Every module still sells alone at any depth.\n\n## Vocabulary policy\n\n",
"Keep the profession's precise terms, because they are the professional appeal and a briefer needs them to talk to designers; drop the manifesto adjectives, because they add heat without meaning; and introduce every kept term by a lab before it is named.\n\n| Keep (precise, professional) | Drop (adjectives and intensifiers) | Rename (obscure for its own sake) |\n|---|---|---|\n| typology, morphology, archetype, affordance, ergonomics, proxemics, tectonics, palimpsest, patina, semiotics, grammar, constant, variable, threshold, anti-DNA, anchor, hero, core, carrier, tech-pack, bill of materials, tolerance, egress, back of house, CapEx and OpEx, minimum viable implementation, handover, provenance, lifecycle, attribution | rigorous, uncompromising, brutal, lethal, absolute, obsessive, profound, bulletproof, hyper-, relentless, unglamorous, seductive (as praise), sublime (as praise) | \"somatic truths\" → the body as metric; \"epistemology\" in headings → how we know; \"ontology\" in headings → what an object is; \"generative world systems\" → method; \"orbital architecture\" stays but is defined in one line; \"Modular\" → Module |\n\n**Rules**\n\n",
"1. A term is first met in a lab, then named, then defined in the glossary; it never appears in unit copy before its lab.\n2. One term per idea; no synonyms for style.\n3. Sentences under 25 words in unit copy; briefs and provocations may be written with more voice.\n4. The original's register survives in two places: the provocation that opens a unit, and the studio briefs at Mastery, where a strong voice sets the stakes.\n5. Every kept term has a one-line \"say it to a designer\" note for the briefer lane.\n\n## Nine angles to read the curriculum from\n\nThe same 21 modules should read as a coherent whole from every angle a learner, a partner or you might take; if one angle shows a hole, the map is wrong, not the angle.\n\n| Angle | The question it answers | What v2 shows |\n|---|---|---|\n| By lane | Where does a maker or a briefer spend their time? | Makers: Practice foundations, Mastery method and one studio. Briefers: Orientation foundations, Practice method, one lens, venture; Mastery only in briefing and handover |\n",
"| By depth | What do I get for 45, 200 or 480 hours? | Orientation: the ideas and the vocabulary. Practice: the method and one medium, usable. Mastery: the original program, built and defended |\n| By the artifact | Which section of the DNA does each module fill? | Foundations fill candidates and the perimeter; Method turns them into rules and tests; areas add the anchor or the brief; venture adds rights, handover, lifecycle and the dossier |\n| By medium | I only care about products (or brands, or spaces): what is mine? | One area's lens and studio, plus the tools it needs (S2 needs T1, T2, T5, T10; S6 needs T3, T4); everything else optional |\n| By knowledge type | Is this history, perception, method, practice or business? | Historical: F1, F4, S-lineage units. Perceptual: F2, F3. Method: M1–M4. Practice: S1–S6, tools. Business and law: R1–R5. Each type has its own research plan |\n",
"| By traditional vs innovative | What could be a normal online course, and what needs an interactive or the AI partner? | Roughly 40% traditional (cases, explainers, briefs), 60% innovative (labs, simulators, the partner's three voices, the Stranger test); the studios are the most innovative part because the partner replaces the fabricator, client and crit |\n| By geography | Is this a global program or a regional one? | Global spine; a regional slot in every unit's world set; the Gulf, MENA and SWANA vertical as the first expansion, filling those slots and adding regional studio prompts |\n| By career stage | I am a student, mid-career, a founder: where do I enter? | Student: Orientation then Practice in order. Mid-career designer: Practice foundations fast, Mastery method and studio. Founder or brand lead: briefer lane, Mastery in R1–R3 |\n| By time budget | I have five hours, a weekend, a quarter, a year | Five hours: one module at Orientation. A weekend: M2 at Practice. A quarter: the Practice path. A year at 10 hours a week: the Mastery path |\n\n",
"**Holes the angles reveal, and the fixes already in the map.** A briefer who wants a studio in identity (S5) has no Mastery path shorter than a maker's; add a commissioning variant of each studio brief. A student with no project cannot run Method; supply three seed worlds. Tools are invisible from the lane angle; show them as a sidebar in every area.\n\n## Alternatives\n\nThe depth-dial model is the recommendation, but four other shapes are defensible, and one of them (core plus pathways) is closer to how professional programs are actually structured; the table gives each its honest case.\n\n| Shape | How it works | Strength | Cost | Suits |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A. Depth dials (recommended) | One curriculum, three depths per module, lanes set defaults | Written once; nobody gets a lesser version; every module sells alone; both audiences share cases and labs | Harder to market (\"what is it?\"); the app must handle depth switching; Mastery content is the bulk of the work | A trial of a repeatable method; a mixed audience |\n",
"| B. Two products | \"Foundations and Method\" for everyone; \"Studios\" for makers, sold separately | Clear positioning; the professional product keeps its edge undiluted | Splits the artifact; briefers never see the studios they commission; duplicated marketing | If the first product must launch fast to one audience |\n| C. Core plus pathways | Method is mandatory; learners choose a pathway (an area plus its tools plus its venture units), like a master's with specialisms | Closest to professional education; strong identity per pathway; natural for accreditation later | Less flexible; a briefer must pick a pathway too; more sequencing rules | If the aim is to look like a school |\n| D. Studio-first | Learners enter through a studio brief in week one and are sent to Foundations and Method units as the brief demands them | Highest motivation for makers; learning is pulled, not pushed | Chaotic for non-designers; hard to build (every studio must link back to every foundation unit); needs the whole program before launch | Experienced designers only |\n",
"| E. Region-first (the original) | The Gulf, MENA and SWANA vertical is the identity; global material supports it | Unique position; deep cultural authority; strong partners possible | Narrows the audience; the commissioned regional assets are the expensive part and cannot be free; parked by your decision | When budget and regional partners exist |\n\n**A hybrid worth considering: A with C's pathways as named routes.** Keep the depth dials, but publish four named routes through them (Maker, Briefer, Founder, Student) so the marketing question has an answer without changing the structure. Routes are just defaults; the dials stay.\n\n**Alternatives inside the map**\n\n- Six areas or four? Keeping the original four keeps focus; adding identity and digital product keeps the briefers, who mostly commission those two. Recommended: six, build the original four first.\n- Tools as a level of their own, or threaded? Threaded at Practice keeps them tied to use; electives at Mastery keep the depth. A separate tools level reads as a bootcamp and was the original's weakness.\n",
"- Method before or after Foundations? Before, for experienced makers (they have a project); after, for everyone else. The dials allow both: Method at Orientation can be taken first as a preview.\n- One artifact or two? One DNA for everyone, with lane-specific attachments. Two artifacts would split the cohort's most useful interaction, makers and briefers testing each other.\n\n## What v2 changes for research, interactives, the AI partner and the build order\n\nThe v1 concept's research plan, free-resource strategy and AI pipeline still hold; v2 adds the Mastery tier, which roughly triples content volume and needs four things v1 did not: studio-scale briefs on free real inputs, larger world sets, long-form interactive patterns, and two more partner roles.\n\n",
"**Research.** World sets grow from 20–30 to 40–60 objects and cases per module, still from open collections; every unit's set carries an empty regional slot. Studio briefs need a free \"site pack\": an OpenStreetMap plan and a CC0-documented building for S1, a common object and a public 3D scan for S2, the learner's own body for S3, a public site for S4, an existing public-domain identity to re-world for S5, a well-known flow for S6. Venture modules need the public guides and open templates from v1 plus real formulas in the simulators.\n\n**Interactives.** The 25 patterns from the analysis all return; three long-form patterns are added for Mastery: a Studio Workspace (the project, its site pack, prototypes, revision history and partner threads in one place), a Handover Auditor that reads a full package, and a Defence Room where the partner's panel questions the dossier. The Stranger test gains a peer mode for the day there are enough learners.\n\n",
"**The AI partner.** Three voices at Practice (Mirror, Stranger, Client); two more at Mastery (Fabricator: tolerances, materials, what will not build; CFO: what it costs, what to cut). Still no grades. The Fabricator and CFO replace the specialist consultations the original assumed.\n\n**Build order** (Practice first everywhere, Mastery where it proves the most)\n\n1. Fix the DNA data structure and the site-pack format.\n2. Practice tier of Foundations and Method, in full.\n3. Mastery tier of M2 and M4 (the spine at full depth).\n4. Practice tier of all six areas; Mastery studio for one (S2 Object is the cheapest to do at home and the easiest to test).\n5. Practice tier of venture; Mastery of R3 and R5.\n6. Remaining Mastery studios and tool electives, one at a time, through the module factory.\n7. The regional vertical, filling the slots.\n\n**Effort, honestly.** At 2–3 days a week with me doing the production, the Practice tier of the whole program is a few months of your review time; each Mastery studio is a month of its own. Orientation comes almost free, since it is the first fifteen minutes of every unit.\n\n## Decisions to close v2\n\n",
"1. The shape: depth dials (A), or A with named routes, or core plus pathways (C).\n2. The areas: six, or the original four first.\n3. Method's place: before Foundations for experienced makers (allowed by the dials), or strictly after.\n4. The first Mastery studio to build: S2 Object (recommended), or the one you would most enjoy judging.\n5. The register: agree the keep and drop lists in §4, or add to them.\n\nAnswer these and I will write the module descriptors for Foundations and Method at all three depths, then the F1 world set and the M2 exemplar DNA as the two proofs.\n"
],
